{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"73651a459f29dd7450c6c7cc39df246c43793a49"},"timestamp":1536875199,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTE2MzczOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2829#issuecomment-421163739"},"message":"[GitMate.io](https://gitmate.io) thinks possibly related issues are https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/227 (datalad get  barfes), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/562 (datalad create   booms), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2693 (datalad aliases), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2828 ([question] Validation and Datalad), and https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1193 (datalad mv).","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536890125,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE4NDUwNDIyMjQ="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536891540,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTIwODEyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2829#issuecomment-421208125"},"message":"Such places where data could be \"put\" (but which are not git repositories themselves, like if you just had a git clone available over ssh) are called [\"git annex special remotes\"](https://git-annex.branchable.com/special_remotes/).  git-annex internally provides support for a [number of data hosting solutions](https://git-annex.branchable.com/special_remotes/) to be connected this way, but not all of the existing ones unfortunately.  The \"most used\"/developed is AWS S3. But git annex allows to extend itself with support for pretty much any new type of similar special annex remote.  That is how we made it possible to e.g. obtain files from tarballs whenever some websites (such as crcns.org, figshare, etc) provide their datasets as a set of tar or zipballs.  git-annex then talks to our custom special remote (called `datalad-archives`) and that one extracts files git annex asks for.\n\nFor google cloud, you would need to install/use an additional special remote: https://github.com/DanielDent/git-annex-remote-rclone  which provides access to Google cloud storage.\n\nThen `datalad get FILES` (via `git annex get` underneath) should automagically obtain them from GC or whereever else they are available for that particular version as you have in your current commit.\n\nOn DataLad side we could potentially\n- provide some (convenience) interface for initializing such special remotes\n- \"bundle\" rclone + git-annex-remote-rclone in our distribution (well, pretty much probably just package for conda, since it might be eventually the easiest non-NeuroDebian way to install datalad. Already works for Linux ;) )\n\nFor the archive on your server, you can already get nearly 100% replica of http://datasets.datalad.org simply by `datalad create-sibling --ui true -s myserver  myserver:/path` if you have datalad installed on the `myserver` and `/path` is served via http. Then whenever you `datalad publish --to=myserver -r`, it would populate the index to be used to browse your repository on your server via http.\nQuick google lead me to some \"3rd party\" notes adopted from our asciinemas at https://www.datalad.org/features.html which might be of interest as well for more details/complex scenarios (e.g. to publish data to myserver while also pushing git repo itself to github):\nhttps://github.com/khanlab/datalad/wiki#4-make-a-remote-to-actually-store-the-files","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536891540,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEyOTg1MDg2Mw=="},"target":"20dfdd07577072769527a0a6c8587a2d5de1aacc025bd7c07171087c61e34e25","message":"Such places where data could be \"put\" (but which are not git repositories themselves, like if you just had a git clone available over ssh) are called [\"git annex special remotes\"](https://git-annex.branchable.com/special_remotes/).  git-annex internally provides support for a [number of data hosting solutions](https://git-annex.branchable.com/special_remotes/) to be connected this way, but not all of the existing ones unfortunately.  The \"most used\"/developed is AWS S3. But git annex allows to extend itself with support for pretty much any new type of similar special annex remote.  That is how we made it possible to e.g. obtain files from tarballs whenever some websites (such as crcns.org, figshare, etc) provide their datasets as a set of tar or zipballs.  git-annex then talks to our custom special remote (called `datalad-archives`) and that one extracts files git annex asks for.\n\nFor google cloud, you would need to install/use an additional special remote: https://github.com/DanielDent/git-annex-remote-rclone  which provides access to Google cloud storage.\n\nIf you specify `autoenable=true` when creating such a special remote, then `datalad install` will enable that remote upon installation, so user might not even need to know (if it is all public) that there is such a thing.  `datalad get FILES` (via `git annex get` underneath) should automagically obtain them from GC or whereever else they are available for that particular version as you have in your current commit.\nE.g. you could try \n- `datalad install -g  ///labs/tarr/face_place` to see that \"To obtain some keys we need to fetch an archive of size 42.5 MB\", so it is our special remote requesting also via git annex to get original tarball first.  Then check out `git annex info` to see that special remote listed\n- `datalad install -g ///crcns/aa-1` to get our `datalad` special remote requesting credentials for CRCNS website, where we would also fetch those tarballs and extract them\n\nWhile crawling S3 public buckets we usually just populate git-annex with URLs pointing to the versioned keys within those buckets... checkout any of `///openfmri` datasets, and `git annex whereis FILE` output\n\nOn DataLad side we could potentially\n- provide some (convenience) interface for initializing such special remotes\n- \"bundle\" rclone + git-annex-remote-rclone in our distribution (well, pretty much probably just package for conda, since it might be eventually the easiest non-NeuroDebian way to install datalad. Already works for Linux ;) )\n\nFor the archive on your server, you can already get nearly 100% replica of http://datasets.datalad.org simply by `datalad create-sibling --ui true -s myserver  myserver:/path` if you have datalad installed on the `myserver` and `/path` is served via http. Then whenever you `datalad publish --to=myserver -r`, it would populate the index to be used to browse your repository on your server via http.\nQuick google lead me to some \"3rd party\" notes adopted from our asciinemas at https://www.datalad.org/features.html which might be of interest as well for more details/complex scenarios (e.g. to publish data to myserver while also pushing git repo itself to github):\nhttps://github.com/khanlab/datalad/wiki#4-make-a-remote-to-actually-store-the-files","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1557913634,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ5MjU4NTUzOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2829#issuecomment-492585539"},"message":"I think this can be closed for now. Feel free to reopen if there is need for more clarification and progress in any direction.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1557913634,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjM0MjcyNTk5OQ=="},"status":2}]}